/Users/c/code/ah-skills/search-demo/search-raw/NeverSight/learn-skills.dev/data/skills-md/team2027/skills/agent-auth/SKILL.md:20: 1. **DON'T**: scrape token *values* to copy-paste (`cat ~/.config/.../config.json`) — none exist until login. (Checking whether `CI`/`BROWSER`/the provider's `*_TOKEN` are set is fine — those suppress the popup; if one is actually set, clear it **off the login command only** with `env -u CI -u BROWSER -u <PROVIDER_TOKEN> <cli> login`, which truly unsets them. Include the provider's own token var (`<PROVIDER_TOKEN>` = the last column of the providers table — `VERCEL_TOKEN`, `NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN`, `SANITY_AUTH_TOKEN`, …): a set `*_TOKEN` is the *strongest* suppressor — the CLI short-circuits straight to token auth and skips the human browser flow entirely — so it must come off alongside `CI`/`BROWSER`. **Apply the same `env -u <PROVIDER_TOKEN>` to the *verify* step too** (`env -u <PROVIDER_TOKEN> <cli> whoami`): `env -u` is scoped to one command, so a session-wide token still gets read by a later `whoami`/`status`, which would then report the *env token's* identity and pass even if the browser login stored nothing — a false "logged in." Keep the token cleared across the whole login-and-verify sequence (or `unset` it in the shell for the duration). Don't reach for `CI= BROWSER= <cli> login`: an *empty* `BROWSER=` is itself a popup-suppressor on some CLIs, so you'd re-introduce the very thing you're clearing — and only bother clearing at all if it's genuinely set, since needlessly emptying `BROWSER` can suppress an otherwise-fine auto-open.) DON'T drive the browser (xdotool, screenshot the X root, CDP, browser-agent logs). DON'T heavy-install an SDK (`npm i <pkg>`, 1000+ pkgs / ~2 min) just to authenticate. DON'T reach for a provider's MCP auth tool (e.g. `mcp__sanity__authenticate`) — drive the CLI's own login.
/Users/c/code/ah-skills/search-demo/search-raw/NeverSight/learn-skills.dev/data/skills-md/team2027/skills/agent-auth/SKILL.md:21: 2. **Get the CLI light & current**: `npx -y <cli-package>@latest --version` (always pair it with `--version`/a subcommand — **never run the bare package**: some default binaries *do work*, e.g. bare `vercel` deploys, bare `workos` scaffolds). This also dodges a *stale* global/brew binary already on PATH, which can have a broken login (an old Homebrew Railway **v4.x** fails its callback). **Binary-only CLIs** (flyctl, render, daytona, auth0 — no npm package) skip the `npx` step entirely: install the documented `brew`/`curl` binary instead, but still `--version`-check *that* binary and watch for a stale older copy (brew `render` ships v1.x while v2.x exists). Sanity-check `<cli> --version 2>&1 | grep -v 'npm warn'` — it MUST print a real version. Blank/error = a stale binary shadowing it (`brew upgrade`/full path) **or** the `npx` wrapper never fetched its native binary (`npx -y @railway/cli` does this → `npm i -g <cli> --foreground-scripts`, or brew). A blank `whoami`/`logout` means the same — NOT logged-out. **Never `sudo`** to install or fix a CLI — on shared Macs `brew` often fails (`/opt/homebrew/Cellar is not writable`) and sudo isn't available; fall back to a user-level install (the CLI's GitHub-release Darwin tarball run from the scratchpad, or a vendor `curl … | bash` that lands in `~/`), then invoke it **by full path** — a `~/.<tool>` install isn't on a bash call's PATH. Don't retry the same install with sudo.
